WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.988
Part two of three: from issue to trusted tests. Part one showed
what changed since demo three, and where Factory goes.

2
00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:17.188
Blue means live. Purple means a recorded real run. Red means
scripted, with no model.

3
00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:26.088
Teal means a decision that the AI Simulated User takes at the
owner's request. Grey means that waiting time is cut inside one
page.

4
00:00:28.100 --> 00:00:37.124
The AI Simulated User operates Factory at the owner's request.
No human appears in this demo, and every review is an agent
review.

5
00:00:42.033 --> 00:00:47.361
A work item starts from a GitHub issue, in a repository that was
onboarded first.

6
00:00:48.833 --> 00:00:55.025
Triage drafts a request. Each requirement gets a check: an
automated test, or a human check.

7
00:00:56.033 --> 00:01:02.273
The request is approved exactly as it stands, by the AI
Simulated User at the owner's request.

8
00:01:03.533 --> 00:01:08.861
A separate agent writes hidden tests. The coding agent sees only
the interface note.

9
00:01:10.033 --> 00:01:15.169
The base check runs the tests on the unchanged code, with a
reason for each failure.

10
00:01:17.133 --> 00:01:22.581
Each test is reviewed against its requirement. A test that does
not match can be removed.

11
00:01:24.333 --> 00:01:30.093
A removed test gives a smaller set with a new digest. The
approved set protects the run.

12
00:01:31.733 --> 00:01:36.317
In demo three, two defective tests were found only when the run
failed.

13
00:01:37.933 --> 00:01:45.349
In this recording, the review found no weak test. All 11 tests
matched, and the set was approved before the run.

14
00:01:49.867 --> 00:01:56.107
Repository onboarding. The setup agent here is scripted. It uses
no model and costs nothing.

15
00:02:02.667 --> 00:02:07.011
The AI Simulated User opens Set up repository for Factory.

16
00:02:07.311 --> 00:02:13.407
It enters the repository, the exact base commit, and the branch,
and starts the scripted setup.

17
00:02:24.067 --> 00:02:28.531
Factory shows the evidence: the attempt, the commit, and the
cost.

18
00:02:29.367 --> 00:02:32.031
Every proposed command is marked Not run.

19
00:02:36.067 --> 00:02:39.235
Each proposed file has a diff against the base commit.

20
00:02:43.667 --> 00:02:53.435
Every owner question needs an answer before approval. The AI
Simulated User answers all three, then validates, approves, and
prepares the draft.

21
00:03:14.167 --> 00:03:21.175
This is the exact draft pull request content. Nothing was sent
to GitHub, and no repository was registered.

22
00:03:24.867 --> 00:03:27.795
The new TaskFlow issue has the label factory.

23
00:03:44.767 --> 00:03:52.639
Import GitHub issue lists the open issues with that label.
Nothing is imported until the AI Simulated User chooses one.

24
00:04:14.267 --> 00:04:16.619
Issue twenty-nine is the new one.

25
00:04:22.667 --> 00:04:26.147
The AI Simulated User imports it as a work item.

26
00:04:27.167 --> 00:04:30.239
Factory creates the work item. Nothing runs yet.

27
00:04:40.967 --> 00:04:46.271
Live triage. A model reads the issue and the code, and drafts a
work request.

28
00:04:56.567 --> 00:05:01.295
The card shows the exact attempt and the money limit: twenty
cents at most.

29
00:05:04.567 --> 00:05:07.183
The AI Simulated User confirms it.

30
00:05:07.483 --> 00:05:12.043
The agent reads the repository files. Each step appears on the
page.

31
00:05:12.767 --> 00:05:14.519
The waiting time is cut here.

32
00:05:20.267 --> 00:05:23.939
The agent proposes a draft, and asks open questions.

33
00:05:24.367 --> 00:05:27.079
Each requirement has a check that verifies it.

34
00:05:37.267 --> 00:05:41.995
The constraints and the allowed paths list the files that this
change may touch.

35
00:05:44.467 --> 00:05:48.619
Each open question has three suggested answers, or a free
answer.

36
00:05:50.967 --> 00:05:55.407
The draft has open questions. Each one has three suggested
answers.

37
00:05:56.067 --> 00:06:00.891
The AI Simulated User reads the questions, and chooses one
answer for each.

38
00:06:05.867 --> 00:06:10.475
Question one: all lists that show an open task get a Complete
button.

39
00:06:11.967 --> 00:06:17.007
Question two: no prototype. The button follows the existing
tab-button style.

40
00:06:18.067 --> 00:06:23.635
Question three: the due date stays, and the task leaves the
Overdue list at once.

41
00:06:24.567 --> 00:06:27.279
Then it sends the answers to the triage agent.

42
00:06:28.667 --> 00:06:32.051
The agent updates the draft. The waiting time is cut.

43
00:06:35.667 --> 00:06:39.963
The updated draft has no open question. Approval comes next.

44
00:06:45.000 --> 00:06:51.624
The work request is still a draft. Before the approval, Factory
needs a decision on the UI change.

45
00:06:52.700 --> 00:07:06.356
The design review. The Complete button is a new interaction, so
Factory asks for an owner-reviewed prototype, or a waiver. The
AI Simulated User writes the reason: the button reuses the
existing tab-button style.

46
00:07:06.800 --> 00:07:09.056
It records the waiver with its reason.

47
00:07:20.500 --> 00:07:27.532
Back on the work item, the approval. The allowed paths stay
inside the repository paths, and nothing blocks it.

48
00:07:32.800 --> 00:07:36.112
The AI Simulated User approves the work request.

49
00:07:41.100 --> 00:07:45.156
Approved. Factory posts the approval on the GitHub issue.

50
00:07:50.967 --> 00:07:58.695
The QA test writer ran live three times. The first attempt
failed after sixty requests, and cost about five cents.

51
00:08:00.467 --> 00:08:10.667
A Factory defect was found and fixed afterwards, in pull request
three hundred and two. The QA request limit was raised from
sixty to one hundred for this recording.

52
00:08:13.967 --> 00:08:19.583
The second attempt failed too, after sixty requests, at a cost
of about eight cents.

53
00:08:20.967 --> 00:08:24.423
The same Factory fix and the higher request limit apply.

54
00:08:27.267 --> 00:08:30.795
The third attempt, now with a request limit of one hundred.

55
00:08:46.567 --> 00:08:51.007
The card shows the exact attempt and the money limit: thirty
cents.

56
00:08:54.367 --> 00:08:56.983
The AI Simulated User confirms it.

57
00:08:57.283 --> 00:09:01.939
The agent writes the tests, and Factory runs them on the
unchanged commit.

58
00:09:02.967 --> 00:09:04.719
The waiting time is cut here.

59
00:09:10.167 --> 00:09:15.351
The set has eleven tests, each with an expectation sentence and
a requirement.

60
00:09:15.867 --> 00:09:20.955
Coverage lists the requirements that the tests cover, and those
that need a human review.

61
00:09:28.467 --> 00:09:33.387
Each test sits beside its requirement, with the sentence that
says what it expects.

62
00:09:46.534 --> 00:09:52.006
Factory ran the tests on the unchanged commit. Missing behaviour
is expected to fail.

63
00:10:04.134 --> 00:10:10.254
Two of eleven results pass. Nine fail, and each failure shows
its reason: Feature missing.

64
00:10:18.134 --> 00:10:22.598
A test can pass at the base. That does not prove that it checks
the new feature.

65
00:10:52.934 --> 00:10:57.878
There is no setup error and no unknown failure, so Factory shows
no warning.

66
00:11:01.478 --> 00:11:07.958
Now the per-test review. The AI Simulated User compares each
test with its requirement, and marks it.

67
00:11:12.378 --> 00:11:16.746
For each test it reads the expectation sentence and the
requirement.

68
00:11:32.778 --> 00:11:35.226
This one matches, with a short note.

69
00:11:36.078 --> 00:11:41.310
To keep the pace, the scrolling between the tests is cut. Every
decision is shown.

70
00:12:22.978 --> 00:12:27.394
Requirement four has three tests. Each is compared with its
requirement.

71
00:12:52.478 --> 00:12:57.326
The last two tests cover requirement seven: the seed data does
not change.

72
00:13:12.978 --> 00:13:15.738
All eleven tests match their requirements.

73
00:13:21.378 --> 00:13:23.970
What happens next depends on the real review.

74
00:13:28.178 --> 00:13:34.562
Every test matches its requirement. No test is marked Does not
match, so no smaller set is needed.

75
00:13:36.478 --> 00:13:42.142
The AI Simulated User approves the exact set. It becomes a
protected check of the run.

76
00:13:43.378 --> 00:13:47.554
The set is approved before the run, and bound to its digest.

77
00:13:50.911 --> 00:13:57.151
Before and after. In demo three, two defective tests were found
only when the coding run failed.

78
00:13:58.411 --> 00:14:06.235
In this recording, the review found no defective test. All
eleven tests matched, and the set was approved before the run.

79
00:14:10.911 --> 00:14:15.615
The evidence of part two, with identifiers, limits, and costs.

80
00:14:15.915 --> 00:14:22.803
Two QA attempts failed because of a Factory defect, now fixed.
Chapter ten found no defective test.

81
00:14:23.411 --> 00:14:28.907
One task on a small app is not a general result. Every review is
an agent review.

82
00:14:30.711 --> 00:14:34.911
The AI Simulated User was played by Claude Code at the owner's
request.
